  "name": null,
  "problem": "Maria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "To express $kP,$ we multiply each entry of $P$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kP & =k[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the value given for $kP$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-right elements, $2k$ and $6.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would get the same result by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements.",
